FILE PRIVILEGES: -----------------------------------------

1ST IDEA:
User-to-file relationships, no cryptography beyond user data and file blobs.
    Suitable for only a server that is run on trusted hardware.
    Files cannot be shared unless unencrypted, duplicated, sent to recieving user, then re-encrypted.

2ND IDEA:
Intermediary db table to hold a file encryption key. has singular foreign user key for owner and array of foreign user keys for read-only data users.
    Suitable for only a server that is run on trusted hardware.
    A good first attempt at key granularity, but severe issues in data semantics still preside. 
    Access revocation is clean and easy.

3RD IDEA:
Using key cryptography to make shared key table data harder to access. I got very confused here and began to research.
    Suitable for only a server that is run on trusted hardware.
    Revocation requires re-encryption of the file data, very messy.

4TH IDEA:
Envelope encryption with wrapper key puncturing. Sources are in sources.txt.
"a puncturing algorithm allows the master secret key to be updated such
that specific wrapped data encryption keys are rendered irrecoverable." - 6.3 Instantiating PFS from PKW and AEAD
    Safe for non-trusted hardware.
    Revocation only requires puncturing of the key that another user has.
    Works great for symmetric key hierarchies, where one key owner individually manages access of many others with their own assigned access keys.
